    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the established patterns of this codebase:\n\n- **`CountOwnerHousehold` mirrors its sibling `CountOwnerNamesakes` exactly** \u2014 same `Count<Entity>` naming, same `service(@Val Owner, OwnerRepository)` shape, same \"iterate `findAll()`, store a frozen count on the entity, derive the tier later\" idiom, and matching javadoc style explaining pipeline position relative to `SaveOwner`. A maintainer would find it immediately familiar.\n- **Pipeline wiring is correct in altitude**: a separate step rather than folding the count into `AssignOwnerHousehold`. Keeping one concern per function (assign id vs. count size) matches how every other owner-creation step is split, and the javadoc correctly documents the ordering dependency on `AssignOwnerHousehold` running first.\n- **Tier logic stays in `OwnerMapper.membershipTier`**, the single existing home for BRONZE/SILVER derivation, with GOLD layered on top as an early return. The right unit, no new catch-all.\n- **The persisted `household_size` column follows the `namesake_count` precedent** \u2014 entity field + getter/setter, and added consistently across all four schema dialects (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) in the same relative position.\n- **OpenAPI doc updated** to describe the new tier, consistent with the prior description.\n\nNothing is duplicated beyond the repository-scanning loop that is already the accepted idiom here, and no class or method is becoming a catch-all. (Note: I did not evaluate correctness/efficiency per the task \u2014 e.g. the second `findAll()` scan or whether backfilled existing owners' sizes stay current are out of scope.)",
